{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the two lotteries. To compute this, we list all outcomes of Lottery A and Lottery B, ignoring probabilities, and calculate the sum of differences between each outcome in A and each outcome in B, considering direction (i.e., A − B). Lottery A has outcomes: 6000, 0. Lottery B has outcomes: 4000, 2000, 0. The pairwise differences (A − B) are: (6000−4000)=2000, (6000−2000)=4000, (6000−0)=6000, (0−4000)=−4000, (0−2000)=−2000, (0−0)=0. The sum of these differences is 2000 + 4000 + 6000 − 4000 − 2000 + 0 = 6000. Since this sum is positive, the reason would favor Lottery A. Alternatively, if absolute differences were intended, the sum would be |2000| + |4000| + |6000| + |−4000| + |−2000| + |0| = 18000, but the reason specifies 'more favorable sum', implying directional comparison. Thus, the reason can be applied, and it would prefer Lottery A based on a positive net sum of outcome differences.",
